WEBVTT

00:06.000 --> 00:14.000
We collect tactile observations together with
RGB, depth, and scanned object geometry.

00:14.000 --> 00:20.000
A handheld 3D scanner captures the object shape for contact annotation.

00:20.000 --> 00:25.000
Each tactile observation is linked to a marked location on the 3D scan.

00:25.000 --> 00:30.000
This links a local surface contact to its tactile record.

00:30.000 --> 00:36.000
We render the scanned object from multiple viewpoints.

00:36.000 --> 00:42.000
Contact-visible views link the same touch to RGB and visible pointmaps.

00:42.000 --> 00:50.000
One object contains many contacts, each paired with a tactile observation.

00:50.000 --> 01:00.000
These contact locations connect local touch to the full object.

01:00.000 --> 01:09.000
We now follow one annotated contact on a hammer.

01:09.000 --> 01:18.000
The highlighted location is paired with its recorded tactile sequence.

01:18.000 --> 01:25.000
The same object is represented by RGB, depth, and pointmap.

01:25.000 --> 01:32.000
Local observations use a shared crop centered on the selected contact.

01:32.000 --> 01:35.000
Tactile images also contain information about contact deformation.

01:35.000 --> 01:44.000
From the same tactile frame, we reconstruct contact depth and a pointmap.

01:44.000 --> 01:52.000
Material labels follow the contact location; one object can contain different materials.

01:52.000 --> 01:58.000
Touch3D contains 505 objects and 20,025 tactile contacts.

01:58.000 --> 02:04.000
Objects span six everyday scene groups.

02:04.000 --> 02:11.000
Touch3D expands object coverage from 100 to 505 objects.

02:11.000 --> 02:18.000
Our ObjectFolder Real evaluation subset contains 85 objects and 2,839 contacts.

02:18.000 --> 02:27.000
CoMT-Bench first tests whether local touch retrieves the same global object.

02:27.000 --> 02:36.000
Patch-level retrieval tests exact contact matching across RGB, depth, and pointmap.

02:36.000 --> 02:44.000
Material and category classification test what tactile features describe.

02:44.000 --> 02:48.000
GeoTAC combines tactile-centered alignment,
DINO supervision, and geometry-relation distillation.

02:48.000 --> 02:54.000
Local tactile features align with RGB, depth, and pointmap from the same contact.

02:54.000 --> 02:58.000
All three alignment terms use touch as the common reference.

02:58.000 --> 03:04.000
A global RGB teacher supervises a local tactile student.

03:04.000 --> 03:08.000
This transfers global visual information to local tactile representations.

03:08.000 --> 03:14.000
Reconstructed tactile geometry provides an additional training signal.

03:14.000 --> 03:20.000
Geometry-relation distillation transfers
local-to-global depth and pointmap similarities.

03:20.000 --> 03:26.000
The three objectives jointly train the tactile representation.

03:26.000 --> 03:34.000
Next, UniVTAC simulation demonstrations show
synchronized RGB and tactile observations.

03:34.000 --> 03:42.000
The head and wrist views show motion while tactile images show local contact.

03:42.000 --> 03:50.000
Grasp Classify shows the object and observations from both tactile sensors.

03:50.000 --> 03:58.000
These are dataset demonstrations, not trained-policy success rollouts.

03:58.000 --> 04:06.000
We compare vision-tactile methods on Touch3D
using the paper's four reported metrics.

04:06.000 --> 04:14.000
Patch alignment improves the contrastive baseline by 5.32 points.

04:14.000 --> 04:22.000
The same methods are evaluated on
ObjectFolder Real without training on that dataset.

04:22.000 --> 04:30.000
Patch Tac with contrastive learning also gives the highest average here.

04:30.000 --> 04:37.000
GeoTAC improves global RGB, depth, and pointmap retrieval.

04:37.000 --> 04:44.000
The comparison includes both vision-tactile and multimodal baselines.

04:44.000 --> 04:51.000
Patch-level scores measure exact contact retrieval within the top five.

04:51.000 --> 04:58.000
GeoTAC leads all three local modality comparisons shown in the paper.

04:58.000 --> 05:05.000
Material and category labels test the information captured by tactile features.

05:05.000 --> 05:12.000
GeoTAC achieves the highest material and
category scores among the methods shown.

05:12.000 --> 05:19.000
The vision-tactile average uses the same four metrics for every method.

05:19.000 --> 05:26.000
The separate eight-metric average also includes depth and pointmap retrieval.

05:26.000 --> 05:32.000
Finally, we compare the paper's tactile-conditioned attention maps.

05:32.000 --> 05:38.000
The three views cover RGB, depth, and pointmap.

05:38.000 --> 05:44.000
These maps are qualitative, not a localization accuracy metric.

05:44.000 --> 05:52.000
Both cross-modal alignment and geometry
distillation improve tactile representations.

05:52.000 --> 06:00.000
Together, GeoTAC reaches a vision-tactile average of 42.10.

06:00.000 --> 06:12.000
Lift Can remains challenging: GeoTAC completes
6 of 40 rollouts, with 20.01% task progress.

06:12.000 --> 06:24.000
On Lift Bottle, GeoTAC completes 5 of 40 rollouts, with 72.38% task progress.

06:24.000 --> 06:36.000
On Grasp Classify, ImageBind, Patch Tac +
CL, and GeoTAC each complete all 40 rollouts.

06:36.000 --> 06:48.000
On Insert Tube, GeoTAC completes 12 of 40 rollouts, with 92.63% task progress.

06:48.000 --> 06:58.000
Across four simulated tasks, GeoTAC achieves
39.38% average success and 71.26% task progress.

06:58.000 --> 07:08.000
Average task progress improves by 11.34
percentage points over DINOv2 (Vision-Tactile).
